WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Alerts should point to conditions that require action, not every interesting fluctuation.

2
00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:18.420
In AI environments, noisy alerts can be especially tempting because there are many signals: latency, queue depth, GPU pressure, pipeline freshness, retrieval health, model-serving errors, dependency failures, and policy decisions.

3
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:23.660
Alert design should prioritize user impact, operational risk, and clear ownership.

4
00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:28.510
Service-level objectives help teams define what good behavior means.

5
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:39.990
For AI systems, SLOs may cover inference latency, availability, error rates, timeout rates, queue delay, retrieval availability, pipeline freshness, model-serving success rates, and data processing reliability.

6
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:47.180
These objectives turn vague concerns into measurable expectations that engineering and operations teams can discuss together.

7
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.230
Runbooks connect alerts to action.

8
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:58.200
A useful runbook points responders toward likely causes, dashboards, logs, traces, owners, escalation paths, and safe remediation steps.

9
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:08.510
It should explain how to distinguish a model-serving issue from scheduler pressure, stale retrieval data, network delay, storage bottlenecks, or an external dependency.

10
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:14.530
Runbooks should also clarify what not to do when the evidence is incomplete.

11
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.160
Incident-ready telemetry preserves evidence about impact, timeline, scope, and contributing factors.

12
00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:26.570
After an incident, the review should improve telemetry, not just fix the immediate issue.

13
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:35.320
If responders lacked a label, dashboard, audit record, freshness signal, or correlation ID, that gap is part of the learning.

14
00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:41.730
Better telemetry makes the next incident shorter, clearer, and less dependent on individual memory.

